{"causes": ["The increasing pressure of Germanic migration and raiding across the Rhine frontier compelled Roman emperors to engage in punitive campaigns.", "The fragmentation of Alemannic leadership into multiple petty kingdoms like Suomar's allowed for independent, opportunistic raids into Roman territory.", "Valentinian I's strategic decision to personally lead a major campaign to stabilize the Rhine frontier directly led to confrontation with Alemannic kings like Suomar."], "effects": ["Suomar's defeat contributed to the temporary stabilization of the Roman Rhine frontier under Emperor Valentinian I in the late 4th century.", "His capture underscored Valentinian I's military prowess and reinforced Roman authority over the Alemanni for a brief period.", "The events surrounding Suomar's capture served as a clear message to other Germanic chieftains about the risks of challenging Roman imperial power directly."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "roman-alemmanic-wars", "sourceName": "Roman-Alemannic Wars", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "suomar", "targetName": "Suomar", "context": "The ongoing Roman-Alemannic Wars, characterized by frequent border incursions and retaliatory campaigns, provided the context for Suomar's rise and actions."}, {"sourceSlug": "suomar", "sourceName": "Suomar", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-solicinium-368-ce", "targetName": "Battle of Solicinium (368 CE)", "context": "Suomar led Alemannic forces against the Roman Empire during the pivotal Battle of Solicinium in 368 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "battle-of-solicinium-368-ce", "sourceName": "Battle of Solicinium (368 CE)", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "solicinium", "targetName": "Solicinium", "context": "The decisive Battle of Solicinium, where Suomar was defeated, took place near the Roman fort of Solicinium, likely modern Sulz am Neckar."}, {"sourceSlug": "valentinian-i", "sourceName": "Valentinian I", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "suomar", "targetName": "Suomar", "context": "Emperor Valentinian I's military campaign and victory at Solicinium directly led to Suomar's capture and subsequent fate in 368 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "suomar", "sourceName": "Suomar", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "macrianus", "targetName": "Macrianus", "context": "Suomar likely collaborated with other Alemannic kings, such as Macrianus, in organizing raids and confronting Roman forces during the 4th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Alemannia, Germania", "role": "Homeland & Kingdom"}, {"name": "Solicinium, Germania", "role": "Site of decisive battle"}, {"name": "Gaul, Roman Empire", "role": "Region of Alemannic raids"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Suomar", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "suomar"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q889668", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suomarius"], "tags": ["Journalists & Chroniclers", "cause-and-effect", "cultural-diffusion", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Suomar's defeat by Valentinian I was a significant regional event, temporarily securing the vital Rhine frontier for the Roman Empire and demonstrating the emperor's military capacity against Germanic incursions.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-11T13:33:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27350429338", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Suomarius was an Alemannic petty king in the 4th century.", "newValue": "Suomar, an Alemannic petty king (rex) in the 4th century CE, emerged as a prominent figure during the tumultuous period of Roman-Germanic frontier conflicts. His significance stems from his direct confrontation with the Roman Emperor Valentinian I, a formidable military leader, during the latter's campaigns to secure the Rhine frontier. Suomar was one of several Alemannic chieftains whose incursions into Roman territory, particularly Gaul, epitomized the persistent pressure exerted by Germanic federations on the Western Roman Empire, contributing to the broader narrative of late antique instability.

His most notable historical appearance occurred in 368 CE, when he participated in the Battle of Solicinium (likely Sulz am Neckar in modern Germany) against Valentinian I. This engagement followed a significant Alemannic raid into Roman Gaul. Alongside other Alemannic leaders such as Macrianus, Suomar led his forces in a fierce resistance against the Roman legions. Despite initial setbacks for the Romans, Valentinian's superior generalship and disciplined forces ultimately prevailed. Suomar was captured during or shortly after the battle, a decisive victory that allowed Valentinian to reassert Roman dominance along the frontier and execute a punitive campaign into Alemannic territory.

Suomar's defeat and capture represented a temporary but crucial stabilization of the Rhine frontier for the Roman Empire, showcasing Valentinian I's military acumen and the enduring strength of the Roman army when led effectively. While his personal fate after capture is not definitively recorded, it is widely believed he was either executed or exiled, serving as a stark warning to other Germanic leaders. His actions and subsequent fall highlight the precarious position of Germanic kings on the periphery of a still-powerful Roman state, and his story is a microcosm of the larger struggle for control over the contested borderlands that defined Late Antiquity."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-11T13:33:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27350429338", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "350 CE"}, {"title": "Rise of print media and public discourse", "type": "Idea", "year": "350 CE"}], "newValue": ["The increasing pressure of Germanic migration and raiding across the Rhine frontier compelled Roman emperors to engage in punitive campaigns.", "The fragmentation of Alemannic leadership into multiple petty kingdoms like Suomar's allowed for independent, opportunistic raids into Roman territory.", "Valentinian I's strategic decision to personally lead a major campaign to stabilize the Rhine frontier directly led to confrontation with Alemannic kings like Suomar."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-11T13:33:33Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27350429338", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Shaped public opinion and historical record", "type": "Idea", "year": ""}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "Classical"}], "newValue": ["Suomar's defeat contributed to the temporary stabilization of the Roman Rhine fro